  they're announced rather than silently painted.

  This is a conventional bell-and-dropdown, so I don't think a mockup earns its keep — but say the word and I'll sketch
  it in a browser tab before we commit to it.

  [1mError handling.[0m Three cases beyond the storage banner from Section 2. Channel fan-out wraps each [38;5;153mdeliver[39m in try/catch
  — a throwing channel must not take down the other channels or block the store append, or one bad renderer silently
  kills notifications. Invalid date input is rejected at the form, so a [38;5;153mNaN[39m [38;5;153mdueAt[39m can never reach the store.
  Notification records pointing at a deleted task are filtered at render, which covers any cascade that didn't complete.

  [1mTesting.[0m Vitest in Node, no jsdom — the four core modules are DOM-free by design.

  - [38;5;153mscheduler.test.js[39m is the centrepiece, all on a fake clock: fires once on crossing [38;5;153mdueAt[39m; a hundred ticks produce no
    duplicate; completed tasks never fire; editing [38;5;153mdueAt[39m re-arms; a one-week clock jump fires everything at once; [38;5;153mdueAt:
[39m    [38;5;153mnull[39m never fires.
  - [38;5;153mstorage.test.js[39m: round-trip, version mismatch, corrupt JSON → backup + empty start, quota → evict dismissed → retry.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications.test.js[39m: upsert idempotency, unread count, read/dismiss, ordering. [38;5;153mtasks.test.js[39m: CRUD, persistence,
    delete-cascades-dismiss.
  - [38;5;153mchannels/[39m and [38;5;153mui/[39m are deliberately not unit tested (approach A's accepted tradeoff). The spec will carry a short
    manual checklist instead, so the gap is written down rather than forgotten.

  That's the last section. If it looks right, I'll write the spec to
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-task-due-notifications-design.md[39m (uncommitted), self-review it, and hand it to you
  to read before anything gets built.
